WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.801
-Welcome, welcome, welcome back!
It's so great to see you!

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.334
-You know what? It's really
great to see you, too.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:06.467
-I miss you, bud.
-I miss you, too.

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:08.300
-I promise you this time
I did not talk to your mom.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.968
I'm not gonna give any
embarrassing videos of you...

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:12.667
-Wow.
-...like I did last time, yeah.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:15.400
No, this is --
-Yeah, we don't let my mom

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.100
have a phone around me anymore.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.067
-Because just of that?
-The last time that

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.334
I was on the show,
my mom sent videos

00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:27.300
that she had secretly recorded
of me to Jimmy

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.067
after I'd gotten LASIK
and was high as hell.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:31.834
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:34.734
-It's literally -- They became
memes, they became gifs.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.234
-They went on
to live a different life

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:37.501
that we never knew.
-They will be the legacy

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:39.534
that lives on
after my music has faded.

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:42.834
-Yeah, right, no, please.
I want to talk to you about --

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.801
-Goggles were taped to my face.
Sorry. It's an important detail.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:47.901
-It was so funny.

00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:50.167
I just want to --
I think you're phenomenal.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.400
I just want to just say that.
-Thank you.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.033
I think you're phenomenal.
-No, but you are

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:55.434
just unbelievable.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.467
There's no --
I don't even know

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.501
if you understand how great
you are and how cool you are

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:00.901
and how phenomenal you are.
But I mean --

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.100
-Can I just interrupt and say,
it's really phenomenal

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.801
to be in the presence of men
and women who have served

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:09.834
our country, so thank you
for everything that you do.

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:12.801
-It's kind of perfect.
They're amazing.

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:16.234
-They're amazing.
[ Applause ]

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:17.767
We don't --
We've never done that,

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:20.367
and you're amazing.
-I --

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:24.601
Very busy two years for you.
During the pandemic,

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:28.167
well, we did whatever we could,
but you put out five albums,

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:30.601
a documentary,
and you won a Grammy.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:33.667
So, I mean,
I think you overshot.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.501
And all great stuff.
-Thank you.

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:36.667
-Quality stuff.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:39.601
And it's almost midnight.
"Red (Taylor's Version)"

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:42.467
will be out very soon,
like in a matter of minutes.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:46.133
Can you believe?
-I'm really --

00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:47.934
I'm really, really
excited about it.

00:01:47.934 --> 00:01:50.501
I've been going through
the process of re-recording

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:54.167
all of my older music,
and in the process,

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:57.100
I've dug up these songs
that I wrote along the way

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.267
that didn't make the album
because I thought,

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.000
"I want to save this
for the next album,"

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.200
but then I --
the next album would just be

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.100
a completely different thing,
so I'd be like,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.634
"Well, what's gonna happen
to that?"

00:02:09.634 --> 00:02:12.033
And it turns out this.
It's an opportunity for me

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:15.334
to go back and re-record
all the music that was on

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:18.767
the original album, and then,
what I'm really excited about

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:21.033
is these songs that no one's
ever heard before

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:23.300
that were supposed to be
on that album.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.067
And I've got artists
like Chris Stapleton

00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.300
and Phoebe Bridgers
featured on them, so...

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.601
-I mean, come on.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:34.434
I mean, I don't know
what I can even show

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:35.834
of the album,
because everyone --

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:37.968
with you, there's so many little
easter eggs and spoilers,

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:41.167
that I don't want
to ruin anything for fans.

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:42.868
That's all I'll show.
-Oh.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:44.334
Whoa!
-That was something, yeah.

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:45.734
-That was bold.
-They just got -- yeah.

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:47.133
So you know it's real.
That's real.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.567
-Oh, my God.
-But this is like four albums.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.100
This is like four vinyls.
-Yeah, and it's not --

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.767
I mean, it is.
It's four vinyls.

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:53.367
-It is four vinyls.
-It's super heavy.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.801
It's actually -- It's incredibly
hard to lift that.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:57.467
-It's hefty, yeah.
-I'm actually impressed.

00:02:57.467 --> 00:02:59.434
-Thank you very much.
I appreciate it.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:00.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:02.033
I've been working out
with a trainer.

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:03.300
I'll give you...
-I was gonna talk to you

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.534
about that later.
-Yeah, no problem, yeah.

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:06.868
-I, uh...

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:09.267
What song are you most excited
for the fans to hear?

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:11.234
-Thank you for asking.
I'll tell you.

00:03:11.234 --> 00:03:13.033
[ Laughter ]
The one --

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:14.701
-Unbelievable.
-The one that I am

00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:16.868
the most excited about
is a song called

00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:20.534
"All Too Well," in parentheses,
"(The 10-minute version)."

00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:22.567
-Yeah, the 10-minute version.
-So...

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.434
-Explain this.
-I will.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:27.067
[ Laughter ]
Basically, when I was writing

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:30.267
the "Red" album, I knew I had
a favorite on the album,

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.334
and it was a song
called "All Too Well."

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.734
But oftentimes,
my personal favorites

00:03:34.734 --> 00:03:37.534
don't line up with songs
that end up being singles

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:38.701
or having videos.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.367
I always just have these
favorites where I'm like,

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.434
"That one's just my favorite."

00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:44.968
But what happened
on this album was,

00:03:44.968 --> 00:03:49.133
this song became the fans'
favorite on its own.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:51.434
My favorite and their
favorite song aligned.

00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:53.200
-Yes.
-And the way that this song

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.367
was written was --
I was --

00:03:55.367 --> 00:04:01.434
I was going through a bit
of a sad time.

00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:04.467
And I was 22,
and I was in band rehearsals,

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:07.267
and I think
I was 21 when I wrote this,

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.200
and I was in band rehearsals
for a tour that I was

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.367
about to go out on called
The Speak Now Tour,

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:16.501
and I showed up for rehearsals,
and I just was really upset

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:18.033
and sad and everybody
could tell.

00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:20.968
It was really, like, not fun
to be around me that day.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:25.434
And so I started playing guitar
and just kind of playing

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:27.200
the same four chords
over and over again.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.634
And I just started kind of --
the band sort of joined in,

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:34.133
and I started ad-libbing
what I was going through

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:36.367
and what I was feeling,
and it went on,

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:38.467
and the song kept building
and building and building

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:42.601
in intensity, and the song just
went on for about, you know,

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:44.868
10 to 15 minutes
of us doing this.

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:48.267
And then we got done with that,
and then we moved on

00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:50.167
to the regularly scheduled
rehearsal.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:52.634
And at the end of the day,
my mom came up to my sound guy,

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:56.267
and she's like, "Is there any
chance that you recorded that?"

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:58.501
-Yeah.
-And he was like, "Yep,"

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:01.467
and handed her a CD.
The 10-minute version

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:05.234
of "All Too Well"
is what was originally written

00:05:05.234 --> 00:05:08.534
for the song before I had to cut
it down to a normal-length song.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:10.434
'Cause that's -- You know.
-Yeah, in case it gets played

00:05:10.434 --> 00:05:11.934
on the radio or whatever.
10 minutes is long.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:14.367
-'Cause 10 minutes is absurd.
That's an absurd length of time

00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:15.834
for a song to be --
-It's like "American Pie"

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:17.400
or something, yeah.
-Who thinks that they can

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.000
put out a 10-minute song.
-We want a 10-minute song

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.434
from you.
We want this from you.

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:24.100
-Well, thanks,
and that's what you'll get,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.934
and that's the thing
I'm the most excited about,

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:28.601
'cause it's the original lyrics.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:30.901
Is that --
[ Clears throat ]

00:05:30.901 --> 00:05:34.133
Is that the normal way --
Is that cathartic for you?

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.400
Is that the normal way you deal
if you're having a rough time?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.534
Do you just grab your guitar
and start writing?

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:40.968
-Yeah, usually.
That's usually the process

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:44.033
that happens.
-No, I mean, or is it, like,

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.300
a journal or you talk
to your friends or your mom?

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.367
-No, I do all of that.
-At the same time.

00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.133
-Literally all of it.
-You do anything.

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.234
-I do --
Basically, I journal,

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:56.033
I talk to my friends,
I write songs, um...

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:58.234
-Well, what reactions --
-I buy cats.

00:05:58.234 --> 00:05:59.701
[ Laughter ]
-Just buy cats.

00:05:59.701 --> 00:06:01.834
-I hang out with the cats.
-Yeah.

00:06:01.834 --> 00:06:04.300
-Adopt a cat.
-Yeah. I mean...

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.000
-If you're
going through a hard time,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.801
they're really understanding
sometimes.

00:06:07.801 --> 00:06:10.167
-Well, this song
is very, very, very --

00:06:10.167 --> 00:06:12.334
obviously very personal,
but it's so beautiful

00:06:12.334 --> 00:06:15.634
and so well-written.
What do you expect from fans

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:18.667
to react to
when they hear this song?

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:22.133
-I really do feel like
I know them really well.

00:06:22.133 --> 00:06:26.367
I go online, I -- I look at what
they think about things.

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:28.267
I really care about
their opinions

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:32.300
and their theories
and their teasing

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.534
and their mocking
and their joking.

00:06:34.534 --> 00:06:36.601
It's all
from a very loving place.

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:39.200
Like, we have a very --
I really like our relationship.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.868
So I think I know
what they are wanting.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.534
I think that the version
that we're putting out tonight

00:06:44.534 --> 00:06:47.734
is gonna be, for them, the new,
like, standard version

00:06:47.734 --> 00:06:50.100
of what this song is, because
it is the original form.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.667
I'm just that proud of it.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:55.067
-Wow.
I got to see "All Too Well,"

00:06:55.067 --> 00:06:57.667
the short film, earlier today.
Thank you for showing it to me.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:07:00.601
-Oh, you're so welcome.
-It is amazing, by the way.

00:07:00.601 --> 00:07:02.334
You wrote this,
you directed this.

00:07:02.334 --> 00:07:04.534
-Yeah!
-And this will be released

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:08.167
on YouTube tomorrow
at 7:00 p.m., Eastern.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:11.400
It -- Sadie Sink, by the way.
-Oh, my God!

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.667
-No, I love her.
I was fan from

00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:14.834
"Stranger Things," I go...
-I feel like people --

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:16.133
Like, we've got to explain
what's going on.

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:18.467
Like, everybody's so busy,
and I feel like I should

00:07:18.467 --> 00:07:20.300
just tell you, like,
I wrote and directed

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.567
a short film
based on the premise

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:24.434
of the song "All Too Well."

00:07:24.434 --> 00:07:27.100
So, I cast Sadie Sink
and Dylan O'Brien,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.767
who are incredible actors...
-Yes.

00:07:28.767 --> 00:07:31.067
-...in this short film,
and they could not

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:33.601
have blown me away more.
It was the most unbelievable

00:07:33.601 --> 00:07:34.968
experience working with people

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:37.834
who are that prepared,
committed.

00:07:37.834 --> 00:07:41.300
Just the chemistry between them
is really on another planet.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.400
-You did a great job, though.
The way it's shot.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:44.834
There's one shot...
-Thank you.

00:07:44.834 --> 00:07:48.300
-...that's a continuous shot.
-'Cause it's a new thing for me.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:49.467
Thank you for saying that.

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:51.467
It's not a thing
that I do normally, so...

00:07:51.467 --> 00:07:53.167
-No, it isn't, but I was --
-I appreciate the compliment.

00:07:53.167 --> 00:07:54.767
-Oh, good.
I didn't know if you were saying

00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:56.234
thank you like you didn't mean
I believed it.

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:58.501
-No, I really loved --
I just liked the compliment.

00:07:58.501 --> 00:08:01.200
-No, I-I -- I really --
There's a shot,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.968
I was gonna say,
this continuous shot around...

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:10.100
-So, there's this scene where
there is one very tense scene

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.968
between the two of them.
-It's so well done.

00:08:11.968 --> 00:08:16.367
-And they're --
They were so electric

00:08:16.367 --> 00:08:18.934
and improv'ing
a lot of what they were doing

00:08:18.934 --> 00:08:21.100
that we just couldn't
take the camera off them.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.300
We couldn't cut,
we couldn't edit.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:26.634
So there's a very long
one-take, one-camera shot

00:08:26.634 --> 00:08:29.934
that lasts for a very long time,
and you --

00:08:29.934 --> 00:08:32.834
and when you're watching it, you
don't -- you don't note that.

00:08:32.834 --> 00:08:36.601
You don't realize that
because they are so magnetic.

00:08:36.601 --> 00:08:38.133
They really are.
-Feels real.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:39.667
I was like, "Oh, my God.
-I'm so glad that

00:08:39.667 --> 00:08:40.868
you felt that, too.
-Props.

00:08:40.868 --> 00:08:41.968
I was like, "That was so good."
Yeah, I love it.

00:08:41.968 --> 00:08:43.133
-I can't wait
for you guys to see it.

00:08:43.133 --> 00:08:45.868
-And there's going to be
a film premiere for this?

00:08:45.868 --> 00:08:48.834
-Yes, actually, and I haven't
actually told people that yet,

00:08:48.834 --> 00:08:51.000
but I'm so excited.
It's going to be at the --

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.968
[ Laughter ]
-I'm sorry about that.

00:08:52.968 --> 00:08:54.467
-No, it's great!
-I'm so sorry.

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:56.000
-It's great!
No, it's good.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.467
This is a great place
to announce things.

00:08:57.467 --> 00:08:58.634
-Okay, perfect.
Yeah, sorry, yes, yes.

00:08:58.634 --> 00:09:01.968
-This is cool.
It's at the AMC 13 theater

00:09:01.968 --> 00:09:04.601
in Lincoln Square in New York
tomorrow afternoon.

00:09:04.601 --> 00:09:05.734
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:05.734 --> 00:09:07.868
That's what
I'm talking about, buddy.

00:09:07.868 --> 00:09:11.300
How --
How fun will that be?

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.334
-I mean, I hope fun.
-Yeah, it'll be great.

00:09:13.334 --> 00:09:15.934
-I hope it'll be fun.
-More with Taylor Swift

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:18.467
when we come back, everybody.
We're gonna talk more.

